{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "956f15da",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Module 06 Lab - Regression and Classification Models**Objective:** To understand the difference between regression and classification and to build your first linear models for both tasks.**In this lab, you will write the code to train and evaluate the models.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dd0e344a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 1: Regression vs. ClassificationThis is the most fundamental distinction between types of supervised learning problems.*   **Regression:** The goal is to predict a **continuous numerical value**.     *   *Examples:* Predicting the price of a house, the temperature tomorrow, or the stock price.*   **Classification:** The goal is to predict a **discrete category or class label**.    *   *Examples:* Predicting if an email is spam or not spam, if a flower is a setosa, versicolor, or virginica, or if a customer will churn or not.**In this lab, we will tackle one of each.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4425ea9d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 2: Linear Regression**Concept:** Linear Regression is used to predict a continuous value. It works by finding the best-fitting straight line through the data points. The model learns a \"slope\" (coefficient) for each feature and an \"intercept\".*   **Problem:** We will predict the `Fare` of a Titanic passenger based on their `Age` and `Pclass`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "5f183e1a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load and prepare the data\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datasciencedojo/datasets/master/titanic.csv')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Drop rows with missing age\n",
    "df.dropna(subset=['Age'], inplace=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define features and target\n",
    "features = ['Age', 'Pclass']\n",
    "target_reg = 'Fare'\n",
    "\n",
    "X_reg = df[features]\n",
    "y_reg = df[target_reg]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Split the data\n",
    "X_train_reg, X_test_reg, y_train_reg, y_test_reg = train_test_split(X_reg, y_reg, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ca8e2dc9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Task 1: Train and Evaluate a Linear Regression Model**Your Task:**1.  Create an instance of the `LinearRegression` model.2.  Train the model using the training data (`X_train_reg`, `y_train_reg`).3.  Make predictions on the test data.4.  Evaluate the model using `mean_squared_error`. This metric tells us the average of the squared differences between the predicted and actual values."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "id": "c35df9eb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Mean Squared Error for Fare Prediction: 3364.92\n",
      "The square root of this is 58.01, meaning our model is off by about $48 on average.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# 1. Create the model instance\n",
    "lr_model = LinearRegression()\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Train the model\n",
    "lr_model.fit(X_train_reg, y_train_reg)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Make predictions\n",
    "y_pred_reg = lr_model.predict(X_test_reg)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Evaluate the model\n",
    "mse = mean_squared_error(y_test_reg, y_pred_reg)\n",
    "print(f\"Mean Squared Error for Fare Prediction: {mse:.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"The square root of this is {np.sqrt(mse):.2f}, meaning our model is off by about $48 on average.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2a75655a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 3: Logistic Regression**Concept:** Despite its name, Logistic Regression is a **classification** algorithm. It works by calculating the probability that a given input belongs to a certain class. It's one of the most widely used and interpretable classification models.*   **Problem:** We will predict whether a passenger `Survived` based on their `Age`, `Pclass`, and `Sex`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "id": "5bee2972",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n",
    "\n",
    "# Prepare data for classification\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datasciencedojo/datasets/master/titanic.csv')\n",
    "df.dropna(subset=['Age'], inplace=True)\n",
    "df['Sex'] = df['Sex'].map({'male': 0, 'female': 1})\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define features and target\n",
    "features_cls = ['Age', 'Pclass', 'Sex']\n",
    "target_cls = 'Survived'\n",
    "\n",
    "X_cls = df[features_cls]\n",
    "y_cls = df[target_cls]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Split the data\n",
    "X_train_cls, X_test_cls, y_train_cls, y_test_cls = train_test_split(X_cls, y_cls, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a8d8e716",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Task 2: Train and Evaluate a Logistic Regression Model**Your Task:**1.  Create an instance of the `LogisticRegression` model.2.  Train the model using the classification training data.3.  Make predictions on the test data.4.  Evaluate the model using `accuracy_score`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "id": "ff0456c7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Accuracy for Survival Prediction: 74.83%\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# 1. Create the model instance\n",
    "log_model = LogisticRegression()\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Train the model\n",
    "log_model.fit(X_train_cls, y_train_cls)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Make predictions\n",
    "y_pred_cls = log_model.predict(X_test_cls)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Evaluate the model\n",
    "accuracy = accuracy_score(y_test_cls, y_pred_cls)\n",
    "print(f\"Accuracy for Survival Prediction: {accuracy:.2%}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d19a0501",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 📝 Knowledge Check\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **In your own words, what is the key difference between a regression problem and a classification problem?**\n",
    "Regression predicts a continuous numerical value (such as price or temperature), whereas classification predicts a discrete category or class label (such as whether an email is spam or whether a customer will churn).\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **The `LinearRegression` model has an attribute called `.coef_`. After you train the model, print `lr_model.coef_`. What do these numbers represent?**\n",
    "These numbers represent the learned coefficients (slopes) for each feature (`Age` and `Pclass`). They tell you how much the predicted target (`Fare`) changes for a one-unit increase in that specific feature, holding all other features constant.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Why did we use `mean_squared_error` to evaluate the regression model but `accuracy_score` for the classification model? Why wouldn't accuracy be a good metric for the fare prediction task?**\n",
    "We use `mean_squared_error` for regression because the target variable is continuous, and we need to measure the magnitude of errors between predicted and actual numbers. We use `accuracy_score` for classification because the target is a discrete class, measuring the percentage of correct categorical predictions. Accuracy doesn't work for fare prediction because ticket prices are continuous values rather than discrete correct/incorrect categories."
   ]
  },
  {
